        <article class="doc-card">
            <header class="doc-header">
                <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
                <p class="doc-meta">Effective 19 September 2026 &middot; SACC Software Limited</p>
            </header>

            <p class="doc-lede">
                Q-Mgr is front-office software built by SACC Software Limited. Organizations — businesses,
                hospitals, government offices and schools — use it to run their queues, front desks, visitor
                logs, communication and, in schools, student welfare and staff records. That means it handles two
                different kinds of personal data, and this policy tries to be honest about the difference rather
                than blur it the way most privacy pages do.
            </p>

            <section class="doc-callout">
                <strong>The short version:</strong> if you're a staff member at an organization using Q-Mgr, we
                are the ones responsible for your account data, described below. If you're someone who took a
                queue ticket, checked in at a front desk, received a message, opened a shared document, or are a
                student or guardian at a school using Q-Mgr, your data was collected by that organization, using
                our platform — we store and move it on their instruction. If you want it corrected or deleted,
                start with them, not us; see "If you're a customer, visitor, student or guardian" below.
            </section>

            <h2>1. Who this covers</h2>
            <p>
                Two groups of people interact with data that passes through Q-Mgr:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Our customers</strong> &mdash; the organizations who sign up for a Q-Mgr account, and
                    the staff they invite (administrators, managers, counter agents, teachers). We are directly
                    responsible for their account and billing data.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>The people they serve</strong> &mdash; people who take a queue ticket, are checked in at
                    a front desk, receive a Broadcast, give feedback, or open a document shared with them; and, in
                    schools, students and their guardians. Legally, the organization is the data controller here
                    and we are the data processor: they decide what to collect and why, we provide the software
                    that stores and moves it.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2>2. What we collect, and why</h2>
            <p><strong>From organizations and their staff:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Account details — name, work email, phone number, role, and a password (stored as a salted hash, never in plain text).</li>
                <li>Organization details — name, industry, branch locations, timezone, and billing contact.</li>
                <li>Billing information — the invoices and payments for the modules you use, and the Mobile Money number your renewals are charged to. Payments are collected by the SACC payment gateway (sacc.ug): Mobile Money is approved on the paying phone with its own PIN, and card details are entered on the gateway's own secure page, so we never see or store a card number. Phone numbers in our payment logs are shown masked.</li>
                <li>Staff records, in schools that use Welfare &amp; Performance — employment details the school keeps (such as start date, qualification and registration number), duty and lesson registers, recognition, appraisals, and the records the school logs about a member of staff. You can read your own file and reply to what is recorded about you from your own workspace.</li>
                <li>An activity log — who viewed, changed or exported a record, when, and from which (shortened) network address and browser. It is how an organization answers "who looked at this?". The name of who did something is removed after the organization's retention window (a year by default); the line itself stays.</li>
                <li>Usage data — which features get used, API calls made through your integration keys, and error logs, so we can keep the service running and fix things that break.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>From the people your organization serves, if you use these features:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Queue tickets</em> &mdash; a name and phone number, if the organization asks for them at check-in, plus which counter served them and how long they waited.</li>
                <li><em>Visitor Management</em> &mdash; full name, phone, email, a national ID or passport number if the site requires it, a check-in photo if the organization's kiosk captures one, vehicle plate, host name, and purpose of visit. If a person is placed on a site's watchlist, the reason given for that flag is also stored — this is one of the more sensitive things we hold, and it exists purely so front-desk staff can be warned; we don't do anything with it beyond storing it and surfacing it back to that organization.</li>
                <li><em>Broadcasts</em> &mdash; contact details (email, phone, or a Telegram chat ID) that an organization uploads or collects, used to send messages the organization composes. Every message we send on their behalf includes a working unsubscribe link — that's not optional, it's built into the sending pipeline.</li>
                <li><em>Feedback</em> &mdash; the answers someone gives to an organization's survey, and a contact detail only if they choose to leave one.</li>
                <li><em>Shared documents</em> &mdash; when someone opens a document an organization has shared with them, we record that it was opened, when, the email address they verified if the link asks for one, and a shortened network address and browser. The organization sees this so it knows who read what it shared; these details are blanked after the organization's chosen retention period.</li>
                <li><em>Students and guardians</em> &mdash; in schools using Welfare &amp; Performance: a student's name, class, date of birth, photograph, guardians and their contact details, and the welfare records the school keeps. Welfare records can be highly sensitive. The school decides who may see them: each record carries a visibility level, class teachers see only their own classes, the most restricted records are visible only to the school's senior administrators, and every export of a child's record is logged. Photographs and evidence files are never served to anyone who isn't allowed to see the record they belong to.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>3. How long we keep it</h2>
            <p>
                Visitor records have a built-in expiry: by default we hold them for 730 days (two years) and then
                a nightly job permanently deletes them. Any organization can change that window to anywhere
                between 30 days and 10 years to match its own compliance needs — we don't second-guess that
                setting, it's theirs to configure. A visitor who's still checked in when their record would
                otherwise expire is never purged mid-visit; the clock only runs on completed visits.
            </p>
            <p>
                The welfare ledger and staff records are append-only: a correction is added as a note rather than
                overwriting what was written, because a record that can be quietly changed is no use to the child
                or the adult it concerns. They are kept for as long as the school's account is active, or until
                the school asks us to remove them. Queue history, broadcast send logs and billing records are kept
                for as long as the organization's account is active, since they're the basis for its reports and
                accounts.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>When an account stops being paid for, its data is eventually deleted in full.</strong> The
                timetable is published in Section 8a of our <a href="/terms">Terms</a> and we email the account
                before each step: suspended for 60 days, closed for 30 more, scheduled for deletion for a final 14.
                That is 104 days in total, with three chances to come back. At the end, every record belonging to
                that organization is permanently deleted &mdash; students, staff, visitors, welfare records,
                documents, messages, settings and every uploaded file &mdash; and we verify afterwards that nothing
                was left behind. An organization can ask us to bring that forward, or for a copy of its data, at any
                point before the last step.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Backups.</strong> We won't claim more than is true. Deleted data can still exist inside
                encrypted database backups until those backups expire on their normal schedule, which is 30 days.
                During that window it is not used for anything and is not available to anyone, and if a backup is
                ever restored the deletion is re-applied automatically so that nothing comes back. After 30 days no
                copy of it remains anywhere.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Two things outlive a deletion, and neither identifies anybody.</strong> If an organization
                ever paid us, Uganda's Tax Procedures Code requires the tax record of that payment to be kept for
                five years: we keep the invoice and the payment &mdash; amount, date, reference &mdash; and remove
                the contact details attached to them. An organization that never paid has no such record and nothing
                is kept. We also keep a short marker that an account was deleted and when, containing no name, no
                address and no email address &mdash; only one-way codes that cannot be turned back into a person. It
                exists so a deleted account cannot quietly reappear, and so we can answer accurately if someone asks
                later what became of their data.
            </p>

            <h2>4. Who else sees it</h2>
            <p>We don't sell personal data, to anyone, ever. A small number of processors handle parts of the job on our behalf:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>SACC payment gateway (sacc.ug)</strong> &mdash; collects subscription payments by Mobile Money and card. It receives the paying phone number or card payer's name and email, and the amount; nothing else about your organization's data.</li>
                <li><strong>SMS, email, WhatsApp, and Telegram gateways</strong> &mdash; only for organizations that send Broadcasts or notifications, and only to deliver the specific message that was sent.</li>
                <li><strong>Our hosting and database provider</strong> &mdash; where everything above physically lives.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                We hand data to law enforcement only when legally compelled to (a valid court order or
                equivalent), and we'll tell the affected organization unless we're legally barred from doing so.
            </p>

            <h2>5. Keeping it safe</h2>
            <p>
                Every connection to Q-Mgr runs over HTTPS. Passwords are hashed, never stored as plain text.
                Each organization's data is logically separated from every other organization's — a bug in one
                tenant's view can't leak another tenant's records, and this is enforced at the database query
                level, not just in the interface. Within an organization, access follows the roles it sets, down
                to which classes a teacher can see. Uploaded files such as photographs and evidence are checked
                on every request against who is asking. Access to production data internally is limited to the
                people who need it to keep the service running. No system is unbreakable, and if something ever
                does go wrong badly enough to expose your data, we'll tell you promptly and plainly, not bury it
                in a blog post six months later.
            </p>

            <h2>6. Cookies and local storage</h2>
            <p>
                We use your browser's local storage to keep you signed in between visits and to remember small
                preferences like your last-selected branch, a date range or how you like to read a document. We
                don't run third-party advertising trackers, and we don't sell browsing data to ad networks —
                there isn't an ad network involved in Q-Mgr at all, on the admin side.
            </p>

            <h2>7. If you're a staff user of an organization on Q-Mgr</h2>
            <p>
                You can review and correct your own account and contact details from your profile or workspace
                at any time, and read and reply to what your school records about you there. To delete your
                account, ask your organization's administrator, or email us at
                <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a> and we'll help.
            </p>

            <h2>8. If you're a customer, visitor, student or guardian</h2>
            <p>
                If an organization checked you into a queue, logged you as a visitor, sent you a message, shared a
                document with you, or keeps records about you or your child as a school, that organization decided
                to collect your data and is the right first place to ask about it — they can see, correct, or
                delete it directly. If you can't reach them, or you'd rather come to us first, email
                <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a> with the organization's name and
                what you'd like done, and we'll help route it and, where we're able to act directly, act on it.
            </p>

            <h2>9. Children</h2>
            <p>
                Q-Mgr's accounts are for organizations and their adult staff; we don't knowingly create an account
                for anyone under 18. Schools that use Welfare &amp; Performance do keep records about their students
                in Q-Mgr, and the school is responsible for them as the data controller. We process that data only
                on the school's instruction and to run the service, and we treat it as the most sensitive data we
                hold. A visitor or queue entry for a minor (for example, a patient at a clinic) is handled the same
                as any other record, under the organization's own responsibility.
            </p>

            <h2>10. Changes to this policy</h2>
            <p>
                If we make a material change to how we handle personal data, we'll update the effective date at
                the top of this page and, for changes that meaningfully affect existing customers, email the
                account admins directly rather than expecting anyone to notice a quiet edit.
            </p>

            <h2>11. Contact</h2>
            <p>
                Questions, requests, or concerns about any of the above: <a href="mailto:support@getsacc.com">support@getsacc.com</a>.
                We read every email that comes in ourselves.
            </p>

